    <section class="section" id="convolution-fundamentals">
      <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
      <h2>Convolution Operations and Spatial Features</h2>
      <p>Convolution is the core operation of CNNs. A filter (kernel) slides across an image, computing element-wise multiplications with local regions, then summing the results. This extracts spatial features like edges, textures, and shapes. Stacking multiple convolutional layers enables learning increasingly complex features: early layers detect edges, later layers detect objects.</p>

      <p>CNNs exploit three key properties of images: local connectivity (pixels near each other are related), weight sharing (the same filter applies everywhere), and spatial hierarchy (features combine to form larger patterns). These properties reduce parameters compared to fully-connected networks, enabling training on large images with reasonable memory and compute.</p>

      <div class="key-concept">
        <strong>Key Concept:</strong> Convolution performs feature extraction automatically during training. Filters learn to detect patterns that minimize loss. This is far more powerful than hand-crafted features and is why deep learning revolutionized computer vision.
      </div>

      <h3>Conv2D Layer and Kernel Operations</h3>
      <p>A Conv2D layer applies multiple filters to input images. Each filter learns to detect different features. Padding adds zeros around image edges to control output dimensions. Stride determines filter step size: stride=1 moves pixel by pixel, stride=2 moves every other pixel.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">tensorflow</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">tf</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">tensorflow.keras</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">layers</span>

<span class="cm"># Basic Conv2D layer</span>
<span class="va">conv_layer</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Conv2D</span>(
    <span class="va">filters</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">32</span>,          <span class="cm"># number of filters</span>
    <span class="va">kernel_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">3</span>,      <span class="cm"># 3x3 filter</span>
    <span class="va">strides</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>,          <span class="cm"># stride of 1</span>
    <span class="va">padding</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'same'</span>,     <span class="cm"># preserve spatial dimensions</span>
    <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Conv2D with larger stride</span>
<span class="va">conv_stride2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Conv2D</span>(
    <span class="va">filters</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">64</span>,
    <span class="va">kernel_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">3</span>,
    <span class="va">strides</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>,          <span class="cm"># downsamples by 2x</span>
    <span class="va">padding</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'valid'</span>    <span class="cm"># no padding</span>
)</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Pooling and Feature Map Reduction</h3>
      <p>Pooling layers downsample feature maps, reducing spatial dimensions and computation. Max pooling takes the maximum value in a window (aggressive, preserves sharp features). Average pooling takes the mean (smoother). Pooling also provides translation invariance: small image shifts don't significantly affect the output.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Max pooling</span>
<span class="va">maxpool</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">MaxPooling2D</span>(
    <span class="va">pool_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>,     <span class="cm"># 2x2 window</span>
    <span class="va">strides</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>          <span class="cm"># stride of 2</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Average pooling</span>
<span class="va">avgpool</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">AveragePooling2D</span>(<span class="va">pool_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>)

<span class="cm"># Global average pooling (reduces to 1x1)</span>
<span class="va">global_avgpool</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">GlobalAveragePooling2D</span>()</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="cnn-architectures">
      <span class="section-label">Architecture Design</span>
      <h2>Building CNN Models</h2>
      <p>CNN architectures follow a consistent pattern: alternating convolution and pooling layers progressively extract and downsample features, then dense layers make final predictions. Early layers capture low-level features (edges), middle layers capture textures, and deeper layers capture semantic patterns (objects).</p>

      <h3>Classic CNN Architecture</h3>
      <p>A typical CNN stacks convolutional blocks, each consisting of Conv2D, activation, pooling, and optional batch normalization. The number of filters typically increases with depth to compensate for smaller spatial dimensions. After feature extraction, global pooling or flattening converts to a vector for classification.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Simple CNN for image classification</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tf.keras.Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Conv2D</span>(<span class="nm">32</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>, <span class="va">input_shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">224</span>, <span class="nm">224</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>)),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">MaxPooling2D</span>(<span class="nm">2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Conv2D</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">MaxPooling2D</span>(<span class="nm">2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Conv2D</span>(<span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">GlobalAveragePooling2D</span>(),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">256</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dropout</span>(<span class="nm">0.5</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)
])

<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">compile</span>(<span class="va">optimizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'adam'</span>, <span class="va">loss</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'categorical_crossentropy'</span>, <span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'accuracy'</span>])</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Data Augmentation for Robustness</h3>
      <p>Data augmentation creates variations of training images through rotations, flips, zooms, and shifts. This increases dataset diversity, improving generalization. CNNs are translation-invariant by design but not rotation or scale invariant; augmentation helps address these limitations.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Data augmentation</span>
<span class="va">augmentation</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tf.keras.Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomFlip</span>(<span class="st">"horizontal"</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomRotation</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomZoom</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
])

<span class="cm"># Apply to dataset</span>
<span class="va">train_data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">train_images</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">map</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: (<span class="va">augmentation</span>(<span class="va">x</span>), <span class="va">x</span>))

<span class="cm"># Alternative: Augmentation in training pipeline</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tf.keras.Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomFlip</span>(<span class="st">"horizontal"</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomRotation</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Conv2D</span>(<span class="nm">32</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="cm"># ... rest of model</span>
])</span></code></pre>
    </section>

